  "statementMd": "Return every subset of the list `items` (whose elements are distinct). Each subset should keep the elements in their original order.\n\nReturn the subsets **sorted by length, then lexicographically**: e.g. `subsets([1, 2])` is `[[], [1], [2], [1, 2]]`.",
  "hints": [
    "Each element is either in or out of a subset: 2ⁿ subsets.",
    "Build recursively from `subsets(items[1:])`, then sort with `key=lambda s: (len(s), s)`."
  ],
  "solutionMd": "```python\ndef subsets(items):\n    if not items:\n        return [[]]\n    rest = subsets(items[1:])\n    result = rest + [[items[0]] + s for s in rest]\n    return sorted(result, key=lambda s: (len(s), s))\n```\n\nThe subsets of a list are the subsets without its first element, plus the same subsets with that element added in front.",
